  m_Entries:
  - displayName: Puffball Mushroom
    prefab: {fileID: 8829377244055881403, guid: 83da7cfce345c3342859735ace0b118e, type: 3}
    thumbnailOverride: {fileID: 0}
    scientificName: 
    description: "Puffball is a common name for a group of mushrooms belonging to
      several genera, including <i>Lycoperdon</i>, <i>Calvatia</i>, and <i>Bovista</i>.
      Unlike typical mushrooms, puffballs do not have visible caps with gills or
      stems. Instead, their fruiting bodies are usually round, pear-shaped, or oval,
      with a smooth, warty, or spiny outer surface.\n\nTheir size varies considerably
      depending on the species. Small puffballs may measure approximately 1\u20135
      cm in diameter, while larger species, such as the giant puffball (<i>Calvatia
      gigantea</i>), can reach 20\u201350 cm or more across. Young puffballs are
      generally white or pale cream, with firm, solid flesh inside. As they mature,
      the interior gradually becomes yellowish, olive-brown, and eventually dark
      brown as the spores develop.\n\nThe outer skin, known as the peridium, surrounds
      the spore-producing tissue. When mature, the skin of many species develops
      an opening or breaks apart, allowing the spores to escape as a cloud of fine,
      powdery dust when the mushroom is squeezed, struck, or disturbed by raindrops.
      This characteristic spore release is the origin of the name \"puffball.\"\n\nA
      key identifying feature is the interior of a young puffball: it should be uniformly
      white and firm, without any developing mushroom structure. This is particularly
      important because young specimens of some poisonous <i>Amanita</i> mushrooms
      can resemble puffballs before their caps emerge."
    habitat: 'Puffballs are saprotrophic fungi, meaning they obtain nutrients by
      breaking down dead and decaying organic matter. Different species grow in different
      environments: many are terrestrial and occur in grasslands, meadows, lawns,
      gardens, forest clearings, and along woodland paths, while others grow directly
      on decaying logs, tree stumps, and other dead wood.


      They are widely
      distributed across temperate regions of the world, including Europe and North
      America. They commonly appear from summer through autumn, although the exact
      fruiting season depends on the species and local climate. Some species grow
      individually, while others form dense clusters or groups. Giant puffballs are
      particularly associated with open grassy areas, fields, and meadows.'
    uses: "Edible in some species, but only when young and correctly identified.
      Many common puffballs, including the common puffball (<i>Lycoperdon perlatum</i>)
      and giant puffball (<i>Calvatia gigantea</i>), are considered edible when their
      interior is completely white, firm, and uniform. They have a mild, earthy mushroom
      flavour and can be sliced and cooked, for example by frying or saut\xE9ing.\n\nAs
      puffballs mature, their internal flesh turns yellowish, olive-brown, and eventually
      into a powdery mass of spores. At this stage, they are unsuitable for eating
      and may cause digestive upset. The mushroom must be cut vertically from top
      to bottom before consumption to check its interior."
    lookAlikes: 'Correct identification is essential: some young, egg-shaped poisonous
      mushrooms, including deadly <i>Amanita</i> species, can resemble puffballs
      externally. A genuine puffball should have a completely uniform white interior
      without any developing cap, gills, or stem. Earthballs (<i>Scleroderma</i>
      species), which can also resemble puffballs, are poisonous or potentially toxic
      and often have darker internal flesh. <b>Never eat a wild mushroom based on
      its external appearance alone.</b>'
    quick_characteristics: "<b>Quick identifiers:</b>\n - Big and round\n - No gills\n
      - Inside should be completely white"
  - displayName: Rusty Bolete
    prefab: {fileID: 8829377244055881403, guid: 537c20698b405314cadbe984b9b4bb35, type: 3}
    thumbnailOverride: {fileID: 0}
    scientificName: Tylopilus ferrugineus
    description: 'It''s name derives from the colour of its reddish-brown cap.

      The
      stem is also brown like the cap except for a paler area near the top. It is
      club shaped when young and occasionally has a net-like pattern right under
      the cap.'
    habitat: It is found in North America, usually in oak woods east of the Rocky
      Mountains.
    uses: Used in food items. Has a mild taste.
    lookAlikes: Bitter bolete; edible, but incredibly bitter. Has a dark brown net
      on the stem.
    quick_characteristics: 'Rust-coloured

      Bruises brown

      Stains pinkish
      to brownis when sliced'
  - displayName: Cep / King Bolete
    prefab: {fileID: 8829377244055881403, guid: 2c8fb8322e584504baa6753e02ac58a6, type: 3}
    thumbnailOverride: {fileID: 0}
    scientificName: 'Boletus edulis '
    description: General appearance, colour and size vary a lot. They have a fine
      white network on the top of the stem right underneath the tubes, although it
      may spread further down the stalk too.
    habitat: 'Can be found virtually everywhere.

'
    uses: '

'
    lookAlikes: 
    quick_characteristics: "<b>Quick identifiers:</b>\r\n \r\nFine white network
      at the top of stem\nDoes not change colour when cut\r\nPores do not show any
      pink tinge "
  - displayName: Porcini Boletus
    prefab: {fileID: 8829377244055881403, guid: 2f5f8f0a2cd14a142bfa10f0ace703a0, type: 3}
    thumbnailOverride: {fileID: 0}
    scientificName: 
    description: General appearance, colour and size vary a lot. They have a fine
